      "rev_n_1_c14f22_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_c14f22_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_c14f22",
        "thought_text": "Check if n is odd. If n is even, it's impossible to express n as m^2 - k^2 because the difference between two squares is always odd.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505231.7494934
      },
      "rev_n_2_afae90_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_afae90_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_afae90",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through possible values of k starting from 1 up to sqrt(n/2). For each k, calculate m^2 = n + k^2 and check if m is an integer. If so, we found a valid pair (m, k).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505231.7495074
      },
      "rev_n_3_cff697_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_cff697_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_cff697",
        "thought_text": "Use the fact that n = m^2 - k^2 can be rewritten as n = (m+k)(m-k). Iterate through factors of n and solve for m and k. This approach might be more efficient than iterating through k directly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505231.7495153
      },
      "rev_n_2_afae90_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_afae90_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_afae90",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through possible values of k starting from 1 up to sqrt(n). For each k, calculate m^2 = n + k^2 and check if m is an integer. If so, we found a valid pair (m, k). Ensure the loop iterates fully up to sqrt(n) instead of stopping one element early.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_afae90_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_f85eb844df",
        "created_at": 1794505243.9594784
      },
      "rev_n_1_c14f22_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_c14f22_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_c14f22",
        "thought_text": "Check if n is a sum of two squares. If n can be expressed as the sum of two squares, then it can be written as m^2 - k^2 where m and k are integers. To check if n is a sum of two squares, iterate through possible values of k and check if (n + k^2) is a perfect square. If it is, then n can be expressed as m^2 - k^2. If no such k exists, then it is impossible to express n as m^2 - k^2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_c14f22_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_5110be46ab",
        "created_at": 1794505262.9279382
      },
      "rev_n_3_cff697_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_cff697_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_cff697",
        "thought_text": "The current approach checks if \\( (i + j) \\) and \\( (j - i) \\) are even, which is correct. However, the issue lies in the handling of the return type. The function should return a tuple of integers, not a list. Additionally, the check for evenness is redundant since \\( i \\) and \\( j \\) will always be integers when \\( n \\) is a difference of squares. Simplify the logic to ensure proper return types and avoid unnecessary checks.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_cff697_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_7c190a27dc",
        "created_at": 1794505279.2151687
      },
      "rev_n_4_cf818b_0": {
